etwork Neurobehavioral Scale: Clinical use with drug exposed infants and their mothers</article-title><source>Clin Perinatology</source><year>1999</year><volume>6</volume><fpage>213</fpage><lpage>230</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B21"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Boukydis</surname><given-names>CFZ</given-names></name><name><surname>Bigsby</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Lester</surname><given-names>BM</given-names></name></person-group><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Lester BM, Tronick EZ</surname></name></person-group><article-title>Clinical uses of the Neonatal Network Neurobehavioral Scale</article-title><source>Neonatal network neurobehavioral scale Peds</source><year>2004</year><volume>113</volume><fpage>679</fpage><lpage>689</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B22"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lester</surname><given-names>BM</given-names></name><name><surname>Tronick</surname><given-names>EZ</given-names></name></person-group><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Singer L, Zeskind PS</surname></name></person-group><article-title>Behavioral assessment scales: The NICU network neurobehavioral assessment scale, the neonatal behavioral assessment scale, and the assessment of the preterm infant's behavior</article-title><source>Biobehavioral assessment of the infant Edited by</source><year>2001</year><publisher-name>New York: Guilford Press</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B23"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lester</surname><given-names>BM</given-names></name><name><surname>Tronick</surname><given-names>EZ</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The neonatal intensive care network neurobehavioral scale</article-title><source>Peds</source><year>2004</year><volume>113</volume></citation></ref><ref id="B24"><c